## useState vs useReducer

## useState — Simple Cases

```tsx
const [open, setOpen]       = useState(false);
const [query, setQuery]     = useState('');
const [count, setCount]     = useState(0);
```

## useReducer — Complex Cases

```tsx
type State = { loading: boolean; data: User[] | null; error: string | null; };
type Action =
  | { type: 'FETCH_START' }
  | { type: 'FETCH_SUCCESS'; payload: User[] }
  | { type: 'FETCH_ERROR'; payload: string };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'FETCH_START':   return { ...state, loading: true, error: null };
    case 'FETCH_SUCCESS': return { loading: false, data: action.payload, error: null };
    case 'FETCH_ERROR':   return { loading: false, data: null, error: action.payload };
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { loading: false, data: null, error: null });

// Usage
dispatch({ type: 'FETCH_START' });
```

## Decision Guide

| Scenario | Pick |
|---|---|
| 1–2 independent booleans/strings | `useState` |
| Object with fields that change together | `useReducer` |
| Next state depends on previous | `useReducer` (functional update also works) |
| 3+ related event types | `useReducer` |
| Need testable state logic | `useReducer` — pure function, no component needed |

## Testing a Reducer

```ts
it('handles FETCH_SUCCESS', () => {
  const next = reducer({ loading: true, data: null, error: null }, {
    type: 'FETCH_SUCCESS', payload: [mockUser]
  });
  expect(next.loading).toBe(false);
  expect(next.data).toHaveLength(1);
});
```

